#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""rastertobrlaser-duplex -- CUPS filter that adds 2-sided printing to brlaser.

The Lenovo LJ2600D is a Brother HBP engine (1284 ID "CMD:PJL,HBP").  The
brlaser driver already speaks that protocol, so printing works, but brlaser
never offers duplex: it has no idea the printer can flip a sheet.

brlaser emits, once per job:

    ESC E  ESC & l <copies> X  ESC & l 2 S  <raster data...>

The Lenovo Windows driver, when auto-duplex is ticked, puts the duplex mode
*before* the copies command:

    ...  ESC & l 7 H  ESC & l 1 S  ESC & l <copies> X  ...

and sends no ESC E at all.  Capture both spool files from the Windows driver
and the only difference in the whole stream is that one digit.

On this engine the duplex mode is latched when the copies command is
processed, so a trailing ESC & l 2 S (what brlaser sends) is simply ignored.
That is why both the position *and* the preceding paper-source command
(ESC & l 7 H) matter -- move the duplex command after ESC & l <copies> X and
the printer stays in simplex.

This filter therefore rewrites the job prologue into the shape above and
passes every other byte through untouched.

Duplex is selected, in order of precedence, by:

  1. the job option  Duplex=<None|DuplexNoTumble|DuplexTumble>
  2. the IPP attribute  sides=<one-sided|two-sided-long-edge|two-sided-short-edge>
  3. the "default" key in /etc/cups/lj2600d-duplex.conf
  4. duplex-long-edge

CUPS does not pass a PPD's *DefaultDuplex down to the filter, which is why
(3) and (4) exist.

Usage (installed as a CUPS filter):
    rastertobrlaser-duplex job-id user title copies options [file]

